On 2021-01-13 17:16, scope845hlang343jbo@icebubble.org wrote:
> Jacob Thomas Errington <jake@mail.jerrington.me> writes:
>
>>  =C2=A0 {mi viska lo na nanmu} is an affirmative statement, that I see
>> something that satisfies (the negative) {na nanmu}.
> By your interpretation, would the following two bridi mean the same
> thing?
>
>    .i mi viska lo na nanmu
>
>    .i mi viska lo na'e nanmu
>
> They'd both be saying that you see something other than a man.
>
No, these have interpretations that are different, based on how {na} and=20
{na'e} are different. Both statements are however affirmative.

For example, {lo karce na nanmu} seems pretty reasonable. {lo karce cu=20
na'e nanmu} on the other hand is much stranger. {na'e} is a scalar=20
negation, so we have to imagine some scale on which to put "nanmu" and=20
then imagine what the opposite of that is. So maybe it means something=20
more like "unmasculine" as opposed to plainly "not a man".

So if I said, {mi viska lo na nanmu} that just means I saw something=20
that is not a man. Maybe a car, or a dog, or a chair. But if I said {mi=20
viska lo na'e nanmu}, I think the interpretation would be that I saw=20
someone who appears unmasculine in some way.

.i mi'e la tsani mu'o

